# Task 4: Email Spam Detection with Machine Learning

## Project Overview
This project uses Natural Language Processing (NLP) and machine learning to classify text messages as spam or ham (legitimate messages). The SMS Spam Collection dataset is used to train and evaluate the classification models.

## Objective
To build a binary classification system that identifies spam messages using text preprocessing, TF-IDF feature extraction and machine learning algorithms.

## Technologies Used
- Python
- Pandas and NumPy
- NLTK
- Scikit-learn
- Matplotlib and Seaborn
- WordCloud
- Google Colab

## Project Workflow
1. Load and inspect the dataset.
2. Clean the data and remove duplicate records.
3. Analyse spam and ham class distribution.
4. Preprocess text by converting it to lowercase and removing punctuation, numbers and stopwords.
5. Convert text into numerical features using TF-IDF.
6. Split the dataset into training and testing sets.
7. Train Multinomial Naive Bayes and Logistic Regression classifiers.
8. Evaluate the models using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score and confusion matrices.
9. Visualise common words using WordClouds.
10. Test the trained model on new messages.

## Model Evaluation
The models were evaluated on the test dataset.

| Model | Accuracy | Precision | Recall | F1-Score |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Multinomial Naive Bayes | 96.52% | 100.00% | 72.52% | 84.07% |
| Logistic Regression | 94.97% | 95.40% | 63.36% | 76.15% |

Multinomial Naive Bayes achieved better overall results on the test dataset. Recall is important because it measures how many actual spam messages are correctly identified.

## Dataset
The project uses the SMS Spam Collection dataset from the UCI Machine Learning Repository.

Dataset source: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/228/sms+spam+collection

## Files
- `OIBSIP_Task4_Email_Spam_Detection.ipynb` — notebook containing preprocessing, model training, evaluation and new-message predictions.

## Conclusion
This project demonstrates how NLP and machine learning can be used to classify messages as spam or ham. The trained models were evaluated and tested on new messages to demonstrate their predictions.
